Exercise 8.26 asks for the translation of untyped \(\lambda\)-calculus into \(U\) by the equations after the proof of 8.9, and for a retranslation of the typed calculus by finitary projections: \(f : D_a \to D_b\) means \(f = b \circ f \circ a\), and \(\lambda x^{D_a}.\,\sigma\) of type \(D_b\) becomes \(\lambda x.\, b(\sigma'[a(x)/x])\), which must satisfy that same type equation. `translateAbs a b body` is \(b \circ \mathrm{body} \circ a\), the abstraction recipe in the hint. `example_826` is only `translateAbs_idMap`, the case in which the body is the identity, so the translation equals \(b \circ a\), proved by unfolding the composite and cancelling the identity, with no retraction hypothesis. The type obligation is `translateAbs_sandwich`: if \(a\) and \(b\) are retractions, then \(b \circ (\mathrm{translateAbs}\, a\, b\, \mathrm{body}) \circ a\) equals the translation, by reassociation and the two idempotences. `translateApp_hasType` adds that if \(f = b \circ f \circ a\) and \(a\) fixes \(x\), then \(b\) fixes \(f(x)\). The untyped equations are `Ulam`, which sends an approximable self-map to the image of its filter under \(i_{\to}\), `Uapply`, which decodes by \(j_{\to}\) and evaluates, and `Uapply_Ulam`, which uses \(j_{\to} \circ i_{\to} = I\) to recover the original action. Those four statements stand outside `example_826`. **Theorem:** partial — the abstraction recipe is `translateAbs`, and the numbered statement is the identity-body case \(b \circ a\). **Proof:** partial — cancelling the identity proves that case, while the type sandwich and the untyped \(\beta\)-law are separate proofs.
